# MULTI-SESSION

## Direction
- One server exposes the sessions in its project session directory
- Session filename, including .jsonl, is the API ID. Clients never send paths
- Transcripts stay authoritative. No registry, index, or duplicated metadata
- Ephemeral state only: active turns, live output, subscribers, cancellation, commit reconciliation
- Thin project coordinator over the existing per-session Service, created on first access
- One active turn per session. One process-wide access code

## Listing
- Public shellfish command, shared with the resume picker
- Recent activity first. Cursor paging by mtime, filename tie-break, opaque cursor
- Paging bounds response size, not filesystem work. Weakly consistent
- Short-lived in-memory cache only if scans prove slow

## API
- GET /sessions?limit=N&cursor=...
- GET /sessions/{id}/stream
- POST /sessions/{id}/turn, /cancel, /permission

## Boundaries
- Validate every ID and transcript: traversal, symlinks, non-regular files, foreign cwd, unsupported format, unsandboxed
- No arbitrary paths, selected-session cookie, or metadata database
- External changes appear on the next listing. Transcript locks still prevent competing turns

## Work outline
1. Session listing command, adopted by the picker
2. Project coordinator and session-scoped routes
3. Web UI list, select, and reconnect
4. Drain every session on shutdown. Second signal cancels
5. Handoffs: fork and compact switch, resume lists, new creates, verbose toggles, server is a no-op

## Open
- Browser URL shape, Service eviction, global subscriber and turn limits
- How the server learns a handoff's target session
- Multiple projects from pre-approved directories

# SECURITY

## Refuse unsandboxed sessions
- Server rejects them at startup
- /server hook blocks with a reason instead of handing off

## SSL termination
- Built in, or documented Caddy or nginx

## Audit TUI, server, and web UI
- Trusted: user code and hooks. Untrusted: model output and tool calls
- Small-scale use over Tailscale or a local network
